# Shell scripts here are sourced/executed directly on Linux (inside the
# OpenFOAM container, on Phoenix/ICE compute nodes) -- a CRLF baked into the
# committed blob by a Windows checkout breaks them at runtime (e.g. "bad
# interpreter: /bin/bash^M", or a subtler corruption of a comparison/heredoc
# mid-script) with no signal until it actually runs there. Force LF in the
# repository regardless of what any individual clone's core.autocrlf says,
# so this class of bug can't be reintroduced by a future commit made on
# Windows with autocrlf=true.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Same for everything else under slurm/ that a cluster executes: SLURM hands a .sbatch to bash,
# so a CRLF there is "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" and the job dies before its first line, and
# fleet_report.py is run on the login and compute nodes too. *.sh alone did not cover them, so a
# clone with core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) checked them out CRLF and
# scripts/pace_workers.sh then scp'd them to ICE/Phoenix -- the blobs are LF, only the checkout
# was wrong, which is why this never showed in the repository itself. Measured 2026-10-12 on a
# Windows clone: phoenix_e3d_node.sbatch 101 CRs, ice_e3d_node.sbatch 110, fleet_report.py 136,
# upload_done.sbatch 22, while both .sh files were correctly LF.
*.sbatch text eol=lf
slurm/* text eol=lf
